Probe the transmitter at maximum attenuation, not 10 dB

adi,tx-attenuation-mdB is the attenuation the driver applies to both
transmit channels in ad9361_setup() (ad9361.c:5243). It is 10000 - 10 dB
- which on a board that reaches about +19 dBm is roughly +9 dBm at the
SMA, against a receive port rated +2.5 dBm and whatever the operator has
or has not attached to TX.

That value is live in two places, and both are exposures.

The first is every boot. The driver probes and applies 10 dB, and the
transmitter sits there until the buildroot init script's tx_quiesce
(patches/0004) writes -89.75 dB over it. The window is short and has
never been measured from outside - the board's own receiver cannot
observe its own boot - but it is real, and it is the one moment an
unterminated TX port is exposed without anybody having asked for
anything.

The second has no window at all: it lasts until someone notices.
DBGFS_INIT (ad9361.c:8312-8323), reached by

    echo 1 > /sys/kernel/debug/iio/iio:device0/initialize

re-runs ad9361_setup() and therefore re-applies pd->tx_atten to both
channels - this board sets adi,2rx-2tx-mode-enable, so both. From a
muted -89.75 dB that is a ~79.75 dB raise, with no unmute, no buffer
enable, and nothing in the log. patches/0005 does not help here: this is
not an ad9361_tx_mute() unmute and never touches the cached attenuation.

Setting the property to maximum attenuation makes the driver's own
default agree with the state tx_quiesce already enforces on every boot,
and makes a debugfs re-init land on silence instead of 10 dB.

What this does NOT change:

  - The TX LO still comes up powered. patches/0004 explains why that is
    deliberate, and nothing here touches it.
  - tx_quiesce keeps its job and its fw_setenv escape hatch. This makes
    it a backstop rather than the only thing standing between a fresh
    boot and +9 dBm.
  - The mute cache is untouched. ad9361_tx_mute() still caches on mute
    and restores on unmute exactly as before.
  - Receive is untouched - this is a transmit attenuator.

The cost is that a transmitter which is never given a gain now stays
silent instead of emitting at 10 dB. Every tool in this repo already
sets an attenuation before transmitting, because tx_quiesce has made
that necessary since patches/0004; the MCP server's tx_gain_db even
documents it ("Must be set, because the firmware idles at maximum
attenuation"). Anything that relied on the 10 dB default was relying on
a value tx_quiesce overwrote milliseconds later.

diff --git a/linux/arch/arm/boot/dts/zynq-pluto-sdr-fishball.dts b/linux/arch/arm/boot/dts/zynq-pluto-sdr-fishball.dts
--- a/linux/arch/arm/boot/dts/zynq-pluto-sdr-fishball.dts
+++ b/linux/arch/arm/boot/dts/zynq-pluto-sdr-fishball.dts
@@ -295,7 +295,7 @@
 				adi,rx-rf-port-input-select-lock-enable;
 				adi,tx-rf-port-input-select = <0x00>;
 				adi,tx-rf-port-input-select-lock-enable;
-				adi,tx-attenuation-mdB = <0x2710>;
+				adi,tx-attenuation-mdB = <0x15E96>;
 				adi,tx-lo-powerdown-managed-enable;
 				adi,rf-rx-bandwidth-hz = <0x112a880>;
 				adi,rf-tx-bandwidth-hz = <0x112a880>;
